Q: Error TS2564: Property &#39;stompClient&#39; has no initializer and is not definitely assigned in the constructor in Angular WebSocket Service A: <p>You just need to assert that the value will be defined, you can use <code>!:</code> to solve this error.</p>
<pre><code>@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root',
})
export class WebSocketService {
  private stompClient!: Client; // &lt;- changed here!
</code></pre>

Q: Is it okay to use setState inside a setState callback? A: <p>Nested state setters are technically possible but not officially recommended.</p>
<p>The problem with code is that it tries to keep <code>newThing</code> reference stable by not using <code>things</code> state dependency as <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79893182/3731501">this answer suggests</a> but still uses <code>setThings</code> and <code>setSelectedThingId</code> as dependencies, through state setter functions are expected to be stable with <code>useState</code>. And <code>deleteThing</code> uses <code>selectedThingId</code> dependency, so nested state setters doesn't solve the problem. As a result, callbacks are still unstable, and depending on how they are used, re-registering them can be inconvenient.</p>
<p>A potentially cleaner and more maintainable way is to use effects. <code>things</code> and <code>selectedThingId</code> are linked states, this emphasizes their relation. Considering state setters are stable, this would require no unnecessary dependencies, but this requires to keep current state value in a ref to access it in a callback:</p>
<pre><code>const selectedThingIdRef = useRef();
selectedThingIdRef.current = selectedThingId;

const newThing = useCallback(() =&gt; {
  setThings(things =&gt; {
    const newId = 1 + Math.max(...things.map(thing =&gt; thing.id));
    return [...things, { id: newId, name: 'New Thing' }];
  });
}, []);

const deleteThing = useCallback(() =&gt; {
  setThings(things =&gt; things.filter(thing =&gt; thing.id !== selectedThingIdRef.current));
}, []);

useEffect(() =&gt; {
  setSelectedThingId(id =&gt; {
    const exists = things.some(thing =&gt; thing.id === id);
    return exists ? id : (things[0]?.id ?? 0);
  });
}, [things]);
</code></pre>
<p>A simpler alternative to <code>selectedThingIdRef</code> workaround would be <a href="https://github.com/reactjs/rfcs/blob/useevent/text/0000-useevent.md" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>useEvent</code> proposal that was intended for callbacks</a>, it provided stable function reference while giving access to component scope. This resulted in <code>useEffectEvent</code> that has similar implementation but doesn't provide a stable reference and the usage is limited to effects. The original <code>useEvent</code> implementation is available via <a href="https://github.com/scottrippey/react-use-event-hook" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>react-use-event-hook</code> package</a>:</p>
<pre><code>const newThing = useEvent(() =&gt; {
  const newId = 1 + Math.max(...things.map(thing =&gt; thing.id));

  setSelectedThingId(newId);

  setThings([
    ...things,
    { id: newId, name: 'New Thing' },
  ]);
});
</code></pre>
<p>Same for <code>deleteThing</code>, no dependency array is needed for stable reference.</p>

Q: Advanced Custom Fields: use a repeater subfield in a shortcode to output another subfield A: <pre><code>function team_list_shortcode() {

    ob_start();

    if( have_rows('team_members') ) :

        echo '&lt;div class=&quot;team-wrapper&quot;&gt;';

        while( have_rows('team_members') ) : the_row();

            $name = get_sub_field('name');
            $designation = get_sub_field('designation');

            echo '&lt;div class=&quot;team-member&quot;&gt;';
            echo '&lt;h3&gt;' . esc_html($name) . '&lt;/h3&gt;';
            echo '&lt;p&gt;' . esc_html($designation) . '&lt;/p&gt;';
            echo '&lt;/div&gt;';

        endwhile;

        echo '&lt;/div&gt;';

    else :
        echo '&lt;p&gt;No team members found.&lt;/p&gt;';
    endif;

    return ob_get_clean();
}

add_shortcode('team_list', 'team_list_shortcode');
</code></pre>
